.TH git\-bisect\-rebase 1 (date) "Linux man-pages (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
git\-bisect\-rebase \- Rebase a branch bisecting to find conflicting commits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY git\~bisect\-rebase
.RI [ option\~ .\|.\|.\&]
.RI [ upstream
.RI [ branch ]]
.YS
.SY git\~bisect\-rebase
.RI [ \-\-abort | \-\-continue | \-\-quit ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR \%git\-bisect\-rebase (1)
rebases
.I branch
on top of
.I \%upstream
progressively,
stopping at each commit from
.I \%upstream
which introduces a conflict.
.P
It uses
.BR \%git\-bisect (1)
to find the conflicts efficiently,
and
.BR \%git\-rebase (1)
to perform the rebase operations.
.SH PARAMETERS
.I \%upstream
and
.I branch
are interpreted as if by
.BR \%git-rebase (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Mode options
.TP
.B \-\-abort
Abort the bisect-rebase operation
and reset HEAD to the original
.IR branch .
.TP
.B \-\-continue
Restart the bisect-rebase process
after having resolved a rebase conflict.
.TP
.B \-\-quit
Abort the bisect-rebase operation,
but HEAD is not reset.
.SS git\-bisect(1) options
The following options are interpreted as if by
.BR \%git\-bisect (1).
.TP
.B \-\-first\-parent
.SS git-rebase(1) options
The following options are interpreted as if by
.BR \%git\-rebase (1).
.TP
.BR \-\-rebase\-merges [=\f[I]arg\f[]]
.TQ
.B \-r
.TQ
.B \-\-no\-rebase\-merges
.TQ
.B \-\-apply
.TQ
.BR \-\-empty =\f[I]arg\f[]
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] keep\-empty
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] reapply\-cherry\-picks
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] allow\-empty\-message
.TQ
.B \-\-merge
.TQ
.B \-m
.TQ
.BR \-\-strategy =\f[I]arg\f[]
.TQ
.BI \-s \~\f[I]arg\f[]
.TQ
.BR \-\-strategy\-option =\f[I]arg\f[]
.TQ
.BI \-X \~arg
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] rerere\-autoupdate
.TQ
.BR \-\-gpg\-sign [=\f[I]arg\f[]]
.TQ
.BI \-S arg
.TQ
.B \-\-no\-gpg\-sign
.TQ
.B \-\-quiet
.TQ
.B \-q
.TQ
.B \-\-verbose
.TQ
.B \-v
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] stat
.TQ
.B \-n
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] verify
.TQ
.BI \-C arg
.TQ
.B \-\-no\-ff
.TQ
.B \-\-force\-rebase
.TQ
.B \-f
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] ignore\-whitespace
.TQ
.BR \-\- [ no\- ] update\-refs
.TQ
.BR \-\-exec =\f[I]arg\f[]
.TQ
.BI \-x \~arg
.SS Other options
.TP
.BR \-\-pre\-exec =\f[I]cmd\f[]
Run
.I cmd
at the target commit found by
.BR git-bisect (1),
before every
.BR git-rebase (1)
operation.
The exit code will be interpreted as if by
.IR git\~bisect\~run\~cmd .
.TP
.BR \-\-post\-exec =\f[I]cmd\f[]
Run
.I cmd
after a
.BR git-rebase (1)
operation finishes successfully.
The exit code will be interpreted as if by
.IR git\~bisect\~run\~cmd .
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Rebasing a single branch
Algorithm for rebasing a branch 'x' on top of 'master'.
.P
.in +4n
.EX
git bisect\-rebase master x;
#	if no conflicts, done
#	loop:
#	resolve conflicts
git rebase \-\-continue;
git bisect\-rebase \-\-continue;
#	.\|.\|.\& rinse and repeat (goto loop)
.EE
.in
.SS Rebasing a tree of branches
Algorithm for rebasing a tree of branches on top of 'master'.
Consider a branch 'A',
with two childs 'B' and 'C'.
.P
.in +4x
.EX
#	loop:
git bisect\-rebase master A;
#	if no conflicts, goto children
git rebase \-\-abort;
git bisect\-rebase \-\-quit;
#	children:
git bisect\-rebase A B;
#	follow algorithm for single branch with master=A and x=B
git bisect\-rebase A C;
#	follow algorithm for single branch with master=A and x=C
#	if 'goto children' above, done
git tag tmpA;
git bisect\-rebase master A;
#	resolve conflicts
git rebase \-\-continue;
git bisect\-rebase \-\-quit;
git rebase \-\-onto A tmpA B;
#	resolve conflicts, if any
git rebase \-\-onto A tmpA C;
#	resolve conflicts, if any
git tag \-d tmpA;
#	.\|.\|.\& rinse and repeat (goto loop)
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-bisect (1),
.BR git-rebase (1)
